{"qid":"Q655621","names":["Principality of Transylvania"],"title":"Principality of Transylvania","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Principality of Transylvania emerged after the Battle of Mohács (1526) shattered the Hungarian kingdom, and was formally recognized as a vassal state of the Ottoman Empire at the Treaty of Speyer in 1570–71. Under the Báthory, Bocskai, Bethlen, and Rákóczi dynasties it served as a crucial buffer between the Habsburgs and the Ottomans, with Gábor Bethlen (r. 1613–29) pursuing an assertive Protestant policy and intervening in the Thirty Years' War. The principality reached its greatest cultural and diplomatic influence under György I Rákóczi (r. 1630–48). Ottoman defeat at Vienna (1683) and the subsequent Habsburg reconquest led to its subordination under the Diploma Leopoldinum (1691), and effective independence ended by 1696.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial boundaries are relatively well attested through Ottoman and Habsburg administrative records, though the exact frontier fluctuated with military campaigns.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Principality of Transylvania (1570–1711)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Principality_of_Transylvania_(1570%E2%80%931711)"},{"label":"Britannica: Transylvania","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Transylvania"},{"label":"Britannica: Gábor Bethlen","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Gabor-Bethlen"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Principality of Transylvania (1570-1711)","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Stephen Báthory","nativeName":"Báthory István","roman":"Stephen Báthory","start":15